WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.050
Go applications often compile into a
small, simple deployment artifact.

2
00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:05.447
That simplicity is useful,

3
00:00:05.527 --> 00:00:09.248
but the security of the artifact still
depends on source code, dependencies,

4
00:00:09.328 --> 00:00:13.050
build inputs, configuration, runtime
permissions, and release practices.

5
00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:16.279
A clean binary does not remove the need
for disciplined delivery.

6
00:00:16.359 --> 00:00:20.309
Go modules make dependency management
explicit, but teams still need ownership.

7
00:00:20.389 --> 00:00:22.336
Review new packages before adding them.

8
00:00:22.416 --> 00:00:25.565
Understand why a dependency is needed,
whether it is maintained,

9
00:00:25.645 --> 00:00:28.908
what transitive dependencies it brings,
and how it will be updated.

10
00:00:28.988 --> 00:00:32.023
Module checksums and version control
support repeatability,

11
00:00:32.103 --> 00:00:34.966
but they do not replace review,
vulnerability monitoring,

12
00:00:35.046 --> 00:00:36.820
or removal of unnecessary packages.

13
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.706
Build and delivery settings deserve
security attention.

14
00:00:39.786 --> 00:00:43.450
Static analysis, linting, unit tests,
integration tests, negative tests,

15
00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:46.908
dependency checks, and CI gates all help
catch issues before release.

16
00:00:46.988 --> 00:00:49.794
Container image choices, runtime users and
permissions,

17
00:00:49.874 --> 00:00:52.336
environment separation, configuration
sources,

18
00:00:52.416 --> 00:00:55.908
and secret injection paths affect the
behavior of the deployed service.

19
00:00:55.988 --> 00:00:59.538
Development convenience should not
silently become production policy.

20
00:00:59.618 --> 00:01:02.595
Secure delivery is a repeatable process,
not a one-time scan.

21
00:01:02.675 --> 00:01:05.767
Maintain release notes, rollback
readiness, patch workflows,

22
00:01:05.847 --> 00:01:08.309
and evidence that important controls were
tested.

23
00:01:08.389 --> 00:01:10.908
When a dependency changes, a build setting
changes,

24
00:01:10.988 --> 00:01:12.534
or a deployment target changes,

25
00:01:12.614 --> 00:01:15.763
reviewers should ask what security
assumptions changed with it.

26
00:01:15.843 --> 00:01:19.793
A well-governed Go service is easier to
update when vulnerabilities, incidents,

27
00:01:19.873 --> 00:01:21.305
or operational needs appear.

28
00:01:21.385 --> 00:01:24.877
Delivery decisions should be visible to
both developers and operators.

29
00:01:24.957 --> 00:01:27.591
A secure build is easier to trust when
inputs are known,

30
00:01:27.671 --> 00:01:31.450
configuration is separated by environment,
runtime permissions are narrow,

31
00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:33.305
and release evidence is easy to find.

32
00:01:33.385 --> 00:01:36.820
That visibility turns patching from a
panic into a practiced workflow.
